# Resolution
Fixed in `f268687 Avoid shard scavenger scan under PG mutex`.

The fix keeps the shard scavenger audit on the same production schedule, but changes the file enumeration path so it no longer acquires a `PgStore` mutex before walking shard directories. `StorageCluster::audit_shard_storage_for_scavenger()` now calls `SharedStorageNode::list_scavenger_shard_files()`, which resolves the PG shard directory from node paths and delegates to `PgStore::list_scavenger_shard_files_in_dir()` for filesystem-only scanning. Metadata row reads still use the PG mutex where needed, but the expensive recursive shard-file scan no longer holds that lock.

Regression coverage was added at both levels:
- `shared_node_scavenger_file_scan_does_not_wait_for_pg_mutex` verifies the node helper completes while the PG mutex is held.
- `cluster_shard_scavenger_audit_does_not_wait_for_non_primary_pg_mutex` verifies the production cluster audit path does not block on a non-primary node's PG mutex.

The fix was validated with the full workspace test suite:
`cargo nextest run --workspace --all-targets --no-fail-fast` passed with `4735` tests.

# Summary
Introduced: automatic background execution of the existing full shard scavenger audit creates a recurring availability hazard. The underlying scan existed previously, but this commit wires it into normal Coordinator startup and runs it periodically in production.
The new Coordinator wiring unconditionally acquires a shared ShardScavengerSweeper for each storage cluster. That sweeper runs audit_shard_storage_for_scavenger() immediately and then every 60 seconds in non-test builds. The audit walks all local PG routes and, for each node/PG, obtains the PgStore mutex and calls list_scavenger_shard_files(). That method recursively enumerates all shard prefix directories and stats every shard file. Because SharedStorageNode::get_pg() returns a MutexGuard<PgStore>, the file-system scan is performed while the per-PG metadata/store mutex is held. A network client with write access can create a very large number of small objects/shards; once present, the background worker will repeatedly scan those files and block normal operations for that PG during each scan. There is no configuration gate, rate limit, batching, or try-lock behavior in this commit.

